When reviewing emails, look for these common warning signs that may indicate a phishing attack:

Ugrency and Threats 
Attackers often try to use pressure by saying your account will be locked or deleted if you don’t act immediately. Legitimate companies rarely demand instant action like this.

Suspicious Sender Address
Always check the full email address—not just the display name. Look for misspellings or unusual domains (e.g., support@paypa1.com instead of paypal.com).

Generic Greetings
Emails that start with “Dear Customer” or “Dear User” instead of your actual name can indicate a mass phishing attempt.

Strange or Mismatched Links
Hover over links before clicking. If the URL doesn’t match the company’s official website, don’t trust it.

Poor Grammar or Formatting

Many phishing emails contain spelling errors, awkward phrasing, or inconsistent formatting.
